# SYNOPSIS

```usage
vx dtc in.dts -o out.dtb [-I dir ...] [--cc clang]
```

# DESCRIPTION

`vx dtc` compiles the devicetree source <in.dts> into <out.dtb>, a flattened tree (version 17) such as the loader reads.
The source goes through the C preprocessor first, the toolchain's clang (or <--cc>), as the kernel's sources have it: `#include` and `#define` run, with the source's directory and each <-I> directory searched for headers.

It reads `/dts-v1/` sources: nodes and properties with labels, overrides of a labelled node (`&label { ... };`) or of a path (`&{/soc} { ... };`), `/delete-node/`, `/delete-property/`, `/omit-if-no-ref/` and `/include/`.
A value is a string, a list of cells (`<...>`) whose cells are numbers, character literals, C expressions in parentheses or references, a list of cells of another size (`/bits/ 8`, `16` or `64`), a byte string (`[00 11 22]`), or a reference to a node, which becomes that node's path; values join with commas.
A reference in cells becomes the phandle of the node it names: a node given one by its source keeps it, and the rest are numbered from 1, past those, in the order they are first referred to.
A node marked `/omit-if-no-ref/` that nothing refers to is left out.

The boards' trees are built this way into the ESP's `dtb/`, from upstream's sources in `firmware/dts/`.

# EXAMPLES

```text
% cd firmware/dts
% vx dtc riscv/spacemit/k1-orangepi-rv2.dts -I include -o /tmp/rv2.dtb
```

